Meg Cole is the sole owner of Cole Precision Components, which she bought from her father Walter Hensley in 2016. What she paid is her basis; she did not found the company. CPC is the great majority of the household balance sheet. David Cole is retired. Barrington Hills, Illinois, and the plant is in Rockford. A competitor sent one unsolicited written inquiry, Meg replied that the company was not for sale, and Nathan, who works in CPC operations, has not been told it arrived. The live transaction: the non-voting units of a new LLC move to a grantor trust [a trust drafted so its income is taxed to the grantor while the assets sit outside the estate] in three steps, a seed gift, then a sale on a demand note, then annual purchases out of the dividend, at a combined discount the file supports with no appraisal at all.


Classroom anchor only. Part 1 is built on a real recurring task from your own practice, using your own synthetic or de-identified data. Do not build it around the Coles — your handoff partner would already know every input.


The corpus for the next three sections: 2011 wills pouring over to a joint revocable trust, the 2014 buy-sell, the 2023 appraisal, and the unexecuted IDGT instrument and note. Retrieval will fail on it twice, in two different ways, and neither failure will look like one on screen.


Case facts are reproduced from the course case anchor and are fully synthetic. No real firm, household, entity or client data appears anywhere in this file. Session 3 syllabus row: grounding principle; note-taker architecture; documentation tools against the confidentiality obligation; recording security; documenting circumstances and basis; grounding vs. fine-tuning; the prep → capture → summarise → follow-up → log workflow; RAG in an advisory practice. Assigned reading: Wolfram (2023), "The Concept of Embeddings" and the opening of "Meaning Space"; Kitces (15 Jan 2025); Iskowitz (29 Jul 2025).


